=== entry 651cc2734134
kind: policy
rules: delegate, store, pr-wait
intent: Translation: workers are delegate_task children recorded by taskId and childThreadId, the program's state lives in the shared per-repo pstack state folder (the Project tracker and store are Cursor's), and PR and CI events come from watch_pull_request and async child completions. Policy (reload-the-orchestrator-after): reload the orchestrator after auto-compaction. The coordinator keeps `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` current; the frontmatter PreToolUse hook records the coordinator's session id once, and the project's settings.json SessionStart compact hook (a compact hook in skill frontmatter does not fire) prints a reload directive and that state file for that session only. references/compact-reload.md holds the settings snippet and script. Keep no scripts, no polling loops, and no relaying between agents.
--- upstream
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `Task`. Keep the program's state in the Project tracker (`notes.md`) and the Project store. Watch each PR with PR and CI event subscriptions when your harness has them. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.
--- patched
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `delegate_task`, each with its whole brief in `task` and its own `clientRequestId`, and keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. Keep the program's state in `notes.md` in the program's store folder, `orchestrate/<project-slug>/` under `~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"` (create it if missing). Also keep `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` current, under 6,000 characters. It is the one file that survives compaction, so it lives in the project and not the store. It holds the standing orders or the path to them, the live agents with their `taskId`s, the open PRs, the decisions waiting on the user, and the next steps. The compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints it back; install that hook in the project's `.claude/settings.json`, or the file is never read. Watch each PR with `watch_pull_request`, which wakes you on CI and review events; a merge ends the watch without a wake. Each async child's completion wakes you too. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.

=== entry ca155dd357b3
kind: translation
rules: isolation, verify-surface, team-kit, model-effort, resume, stop, same-model-review
intent: Workers that write get their own worktree on this machine, the way upstream defaulted them to a cloud VM, and the list of reasons to stay local becomes a note that every worker runs here and can reach the verification surface. Forking has no T3 primitive, so new work that needs a worker's built state goes to a new worker pointed at that worker's worktree once it has finished, keeping one writer per worktree (no rule covers fork). A follow-up message is t3_thread_send to the childThreadId, and task_status checks a worker without waking it. Policy: the verifier runs on the owner's model and effort (rule same-model-review). Preserve one owner per PR, the three-level cap, the rolling window of about ten, never messaging to check on an agent, and starting with one unit.
--- upstream
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run workers on a new cloud VM (`environment: "cloud"`) unless the work needs this machine: a `control-ui` or `control-cli` runtime check (from `cursor-team-kit`), local transcripts, a simulator, or auth that exists only here. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on a different model family. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
--- patched
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. A worker that writes gets its own worktree (`isolation: "worktree"`), a checkout of your current local commit, so commit first or have its kickoff check out a pushed branch. Every worker runs on this machine, so a runtime check through the project's own verification skill (otherwise T3's `preview_*` tools for a UI, or the shell for a CLI), local transcripts, a simulator, and auth that exists only here are all in reach. Set each subagent's effort for the job, lowering its line's effort for routine work and never raising it, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **When new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process, give it to a new worker whose kickoff names that worker's worktree path, once the first worker has finished. Message a worker (`t3_thread_send` to its `childThreadId`) only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent. `task_status` reads its state without waking it.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on the same model and effort as the owner it checks, unless the user named another. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.

=== entry b0d7f0bf067a
kind: translation
rules: stop
intent: Stopping a stalled or looping worker is `task_cancel`, which also stops what it delegated, confirmed with `task_status`. Judging it by pushes and PR activity, the retry-by-cause list, the two-retry cap, and handling a late return stay verbatim, as does the rest of the Supervision list.
--- upstream
- **Each kickoff names the evidence that proves the work is done,** such as a command and its expected output, a control-skill check, or an artifact. It also names the goal, the paths the worker may and may not write, and the actions it must not take. If you can't name the evidence, the unit isn't scoped yet.
- **Read the diff or the artifact, not only the worker's summary.** Green CI is an input to a verdict, not a verdict. Behavioral work needs more than a type check. A verdict covers one head SHA, so a new push needs a new verdict. Read a sample of each sub-coordinator's kickoffs while its workers run, and fix its instructions when one fails.
- **Send back work that drifts from scope or grows past what was asked.** Keep only the fixes that block the program, and park the rest as follow-ups.
- **Check a PR's live state before you report on it,** with `gh pr view` and `gh pr checks` or your forge's equivalents, not the worker's last report. Account for every worker you spawned as done, replaced, or absorbed into another worker's scope.
- **Stop or replace a worker that stalls or loops.** Judge it by its pushes and PR activity, not its status text. Retry by cause: a smaller scope after a context or memory limit, the same kickoff after a dropped connection, and a different model after a tool error. After two retries, drop the unit and replan around it. When a stopped worker returns late, check its result against the PR's current state, and hand any unique findings to a fresh worker.
- **Ask the user only for a real decision or a blocker,** such as an irreversible action, a product or preference call that no experiment settles, or a dead end that survived a replan. Rebase mechanics, retries, CI flakes, review triage, and "should I keep going" never reach the user.
--- patched
- **Each kickoff names the evidence that proves the work is done,** such as a command and its expected output, a control-skill check, or an artifact. It also names the goal, the paths the worker may and may not write, and the actions it must not take. If you can't name the evidence, the unit isn't scoped yet.
- **Read the diff or the artifact, not only the worker's summary.** Green CI is an input to a verdict, not a verdict. Behavioral work needs more than a type check. A verdict covers one head SHA, so a new push needs a new verdict. Read a sample of each sub-coordinator's kickoffs while its workers run, and fix its instructions when one fails.
- **Send back work that drifts from scope or grows past what was asked.** Keep only the fixes that block the program, and park the rest as follow-ups.
- **Check a PR's live state before you report on it,** with `gh pr view` and `gh pr checks` or your forge's equivalents, not the worker's last report. Account for every worker you spawned as done, replaced, or absorbed into another worker's scope.
- **Stop or replace a worker that stalls or loops.** Stop it with `task_cancel`, which also stops the tasks it delegated, then confirm with `task_status`. Judge it by its pushes and PR activity, not its status text. Retry by cause: a smaller scope after a context or memory limit, the same kickoff after a dropped connection, and a different model after a tool error. After two retries, drop the unit and replan around it. When a stopped worker returns late, check its result against the PR's current state, and hand any unique findings to a fresh worker.
- **Ask the user only for a real decision or a blocker,** such as an irreversible action, a product or preference call that no experiment settles, or a dead end that survived a replan. Rebase mechanics, retries, CI flakes, review triage, and "should I keep going" never reach the user.
